<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Sixtus IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1414">1414</a>–<a href="/years/1484">1484</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Francesco della Rovere · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/della-rovere">Della Rovere</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Sixtus IV reigned from 1471 to 1484. He built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a> and refounded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Vatican Library</a>, but was notorious for advancing his nephews, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girolamo-riario">Girolamo Riario</a>, for whom he acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imola">Imola</a> and Forlì. His family's hostility to the Medici lay behind the Pazzi conspiracy of 1478.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv.jpg" alt="Pope Sixtus IV" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pedro Berruguete · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_Berruguete_-_Portrait_of_Pope_Sixtus_IV_della_Rovere_-_1916.815_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_ArtFXD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Francesco della Rovere was born in Celle Ligure, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savona">Savona</a>, the son of Leonardo Beltramo di Savona della Rovere and Luchina Monteleoni. Accounts of his family differ: Mandell Creighton holds that his origin was so lowly that he lacked a surname and took the name from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> family he tutored. At the age of nine he joined the Franciscan Order. He studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, received his doctorate at Padua in 1444 and lectured at several Italian universities. In 1464 he was elected Minister General of the Franciscan order, and in 1467 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-ii">Pope Paul II</a> made him a cardinal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His reputation for piety helped the cardinals to choose him as pope on the unexpected death of Paul II, and he took the name Sixtus on 9 August 1471. He declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the Ottoman Turks, but the fleet disbanded after the conquest of Smyrna, and he turned to temporal and dynastic matters. Six of the 34 cardinals he created were nephews. Pietro Riario rose to be a cardinal and the effective ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> until his death in 1474. Sixtus was personally involved in the Pazzi conspiracy of 1478 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo de' Medici</a> and his brother Giuliano, and answered its failure with an interdict and two years of war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>.</p><p>On 1 November 1478 he issued the bull establishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, under pressure from Ferdinand of Aragon, and in 1482 he condemned its worst abuses. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> he restored the aqueduct of the Acqua Vergine, rebuilt or restored over 30 churches, sponsored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a> and the Ponte Sisto, and began the first paving since antiquity. He donated Roman sculptures that founded a papal art collection, refounded and enlarged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Vatican Library</a>, and in 1477 authorised the creation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala-university">Uppsala University</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sixtus fell ill on 8 August 1484 and died on the evening of 12 August. The Medici envoy announced it with the words 'may God forgive him'. His tomb was destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a> in 1527. His bronze funerary monument by Antonio del Pollaiuolo, completed by 1493, is now in the Treasury of St Peter's Basilica. Even the hostile chronicler Stefano Infessura agreed that his work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> deserved admiration.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Sixtus IV” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1481 <b>Pope Sixtus IV</b>, at the request of Edward IV, issued a bull of neutrality that protected the Channel Islands and ships in their waters during wars between England and France, a privilege that lasted until 1689.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Bailiwick of Jersey · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Spanish Inquisition, authorised by <b>Pope Sixtus IV</b> in 1478, became a royal tribunal answerable to the crown rather than to Rome.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Renaissance and Baroque popes, Rome became the artistic capital of Europe: <b>Sixtus IV</b> built the Sistine Chapel and founded the Vatican Library, Julius II commissioned Michelangelo’s ceiling of the Sistine Chapel and Raphael’s frescoes in the Vatican, and the new basilica of Saint Peter, begun in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Politically, Stephen the Great’s resistance to the Ottomans made him a national hero, and <b>Pope Sixtus IV</b> praised him as an “athlete of Christ”.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sistine Chapel is the large papal chapel built within the Vatican between 1477 and 1480 by <b>Pope Sixtus IV</b>, for whom it is named.</blockquote><a href="/work/sistine-chapel-ceiling">Sistine Chapel ceiling · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Pino III Ordelaffi died in 1480, and <b>Pope Sixtus IV</b> granted Forlì to his nephew Girolamo Riario, joining it to the Lordship of Imola and Forli.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-forli">Lordship of Forli</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Celle Ligure, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.34,8.55&amp;year=1459" aria-label="Celle Ligure on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pope Sixtus IV"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5521 3767h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5521 3767h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.34,8.55&amp;year=1459">What was here in 1459? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
